              "id": "e642ad2a",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "html",
              "isInner": false,
              "settings": {
                "html": "<section class=\"sec-editorial bg-img-truth text-center\">\n    <div class=\"overlay-layer overlay-crimson-alert\"></div>\n    <div class=\"container\" style=\"max-width: 950px;\">\n      <div class=\"eyebrow-badge\" style=\"background: var(--crimson-bg); color: var(--crimson-fail); border-color: #fca5a5;\">THE UNCOMFORTABLE TRUTH</div>\n      <h2 class=\"sec-title\">Let's start with what the other \"AI book\" tools won't tell you.</h2>\n      <div style=\"font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--ink-body); text-align: left; line-height: 1.8;\">\n        <p style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n          Right now, dozens of tools promise to spit out a complete book in \"6 minutes\" or \"60 seconds.\" Sounds amazing. Here's the problem: that speed comes from one-shot AI text that reads like exactly what it is — filler. And Amazon has noticed.\n        </p>\n        <p style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; font-weight: 800; color: var(--crimson-fail); background: #fff5f5; padding: 18px 24px; border-left: 5px solid var(--crimson-fail); border-radius: 8px;\">\n          AI-detection studies, major press (Seattle Times, Wired), and Amazon's own tightening rules are all pointing the same direction: <strong>low-quality AI books are getting flagged, rejected, refunded, and removed.</strong> The \"6-minute book\" isn't a shortcut anymore. It's a liability.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </section>",
                "_element_id": "section-9-the-uncomfortable-truth"
              },
